# Exam track — Theme: Describing a Picture — Sub-lesson 3: Weather Description
#
# The WÉINI task for the bus-stop photo of sub-lessons 1–2: the time of day, and a
# hedged guess at the season. The examiner asks it on almost every picture, and it
# is the one place in this theme where the candidate is allowed to INFER rather
# than report — so the point of this file is the hedging vocabulary, not the
# weather words.
#
# This photo's inference angle is new relative to the other four themes: the
# evidence points TWO WAYS AT ONCE. The bare trees argue for winter, the same as
# groussgaass — but unlike groussgaass, nobody here is in a thick coat: the older
# man's coat is a light beige, one woman is bare-legged in a dress, no one wears a
# hat or gloves. A learner who has played groussgaass must not just recall "bare
# trees → winter"; they have to weigh the CLOTHING evidence against the TREE
# evidence and conclude the milder answer, spring. That is the theme's actual
# teaching point — hedging is for when the evidence conflicts, not just when it
# is absent (chreschtmaart) or consistent (groussgaass).
#
# The second inference: the sky is bright and overcast with no visible sun, no
# rain, no strong shadows — daytime, most likely around midday. And the crowd
# itself is evidence for an ORDINARY weekday: people waiting at a bus stop, in
# ones and twos, is what a normal commute looks like, unlike chreschtmaart's
# festive gathering. This mirrors groussgaass's "vill Leit ënnerwee" weekday
# inference, but grounded in a different, equally checkable fact: people waiting
# for public transport, presumably on their way to work.
#
# The photo detail this describes: a pale, bright, overcast sky with no visible
# sun and no rain; bare trees; the older man in a light beige coat, no hat or
# gloves visible on anyone, one woman in a dress with bare legs — evidence against
# deep winter despite the bare trees.
#
# PURE DESCRIPTION, same as 01 and 02 — no @question prompts, no first-person
# opinion. A hedged inference *about the photo* ("I think it is spring, because
# the people are not wearing thick coats") is not an opinion: it is an observation
# with a confidence marker, and a stranger can still check it against the picture.
#
# Same A1–A2 constraint as the rest of the theme: present tense, simple main
# clauses or short two-clause sentences, no attributive adjectives on masculine
# or neuter nouns.
#
# Words sub-lessons 1–2 already teach (d'Bild, d'Leit, de Busarrêt, gesinn, …) are
# deliberately NOT repeated — the pass-gate makes them prerequisites and stat keys
# are shared across the app.
#
# Ten @fill blocks — this theme's highest-value frames, because a hedge is a
# pattern that transfers to every picture and every topic. The contradictory-
# evidence pair (`Ech mengen, … well d'Leit keng décke Mäntel undoen` and its
# `mee`-contrast companion) is this file's new contribution to the library; the
# rest reuse `Ech sinn net sécher, mee villäicht`, `Et kéint … sinn`, `Ech weess
# net, ob …`, `bis` (a natural fit for a bus stop), and `obwuel` on fresh
# vocabulary. See the skill's references/content-contract.md for the mechanized
# bounds.

@lesson P4.03 "Busarrêt: Weather Description"

# Same photo as 01, declared again: @image is per-file, and the learner must see
# the photo during THIS sub-lesson too. The alt text differs on purpose — it
# describes the detail this file drills.
@image "/assets/exam/picture/busarret/img/busarret.webp"
@image-alt "The same bus-stop photo, seen for the weather: a pale, bright overcast sky with no visible sun and no rain, bare trees, and people in light coats and no hats — evidence against deep winter despite the bare trees."

# --- Weather and sky ---
@word d'Wieder = the weather
@word den Himmel = the sky
@word d'Sonn = the sun
@word d'Wolleken = the clouds
@word bedeckt = overcast
@word reenen = to rain

# --- Time and season words ---
@word den Dag = the day
@word de Mëtteg = the afternoon
@word mëttes = in the afternoon
@word d'Woch = the week
@word besonnesch = special
@word d'Blieder = the leaves

# --- Hedging a guess ---
@word mengen = to think
@word warscheinlech = probably
@word sécher = sure
@word well = because
@word schaffen = to work

# --- Seasons (contrast for the hedges) ---
@word de Wanter = the winter
@word de Summer = the summer
@word d'Fréijoer = the spring

@sentence
@lu Den Himmel ass gro a bedeckt.
@en The sky is grey and overcast.
@distractor-lu blo
@distractor-lu Sonn
@distractor-en blue
@distractor-en sun

@sentence
@lu Et reent net, mee et ass frësch.
@en It is not raining, but it is fresh.
@distractor-lu waarm
@distractor-lu Wollek
@distractor-en warm
@distractor-en cloud

@sentence
@lu D'Beem hu keng Blieder.
@en The trees have no leaves.
@distractor-lu Autoen
@distractor-lu Bus
@distractor-en cars
@distractor-en bus

@sentence
@lu Ech mengen, et ass mëttes.
@en I think it is afternoon.
@distractor-lu nuets
@distractor-lu Owend
@distractor-en at night
@distractor-en evening

@sentence
@lu Vill Leit waarden op de Bus, well si schaffen.
@en A lot of people are waiting for the bus, because they work.
@distractor-lu spillen
@distractor-lu Kanner
@distractor-en play
@distractor-en children

@sentence
@lu Den Dag ass warscheinlech normal a net besonnesch.
@en The day is probably normal and not special.
@distractor-lu kal
@distractor-lu gro
@distractor-en cold
@distractor-en grey

# --- Reusable frames (@fill) ---

# The contradictory-evidence hedge — this file's new contribution to the library.
# `Ech mengen, …` opens as usual, but the justification now needs BOTH facts, not
# one: bare trees alone would suggest `Wanter`, and no thick coats alone would
# suggest `Summer` just as well — only stating both together, contrasted with
# `mee`, rules out each distractor from a different half of the sentence. The
# comma before the blank exempts it from the Eifeler Regel.
@fill
@lu Ech mengen, et ass [Fréijoer], well d'Beem keng Blieder hunn, mee d'Leit keng décke Mäntel undoen.
@en I think it is [spring], because the trees have no leaves, but the people are not wearing thick coats.
@distractor-lu Wanter
@distractor-lu Summer
@distractor-en winter
@distractor-en summer

# The `mee`-contrast companion to the frame above: the bare-trees evidence stated
# plainly, then contradicted. `mee` takes no inversion, so this is the no-inversion
# half of the connector pair the `wann`/`obwuel` frames elsewhere complete.
@fill
@lu D'Beem hu keng [Blieder], mee et ass net kal.
@en The trees have no [leaves], but it is not cold.
@distractor-lu Wolleken
@distractor-lu Autoen
@distractor-en clouds
@distractor-en cars

# Hedge: `Ech sinn net sécher, mee villäicht` — the strongest disclaimer in the
# set, used here for the time of day rather than a season guess, since the flat
# light gives little to read. The comma both marks the clause boundary and
# exempts the blank from the Eifeler Regel.
@fill
@lu Ech sinn net sécher, mee villäicht ass et [Mëtteg].
@en I am not sure, but maybe it is [midday].
@distractor-lu Owend
@distractor-lu Nuecht
@distractor-en evening
@distractor-en night

# Hedge: `Et kéint … sinn`, the subjunctive hedge, mirrored from groussgaass with
# the blank moved past the fixed `en normalen Dag` — the declined attributive
# stays whole in the frame (never the blank itself), and `der` before the blank
# admits only a feminine tile, ruling out masculine `Chrëschtmount` on grammar
# alone (R4) as well as on the photo.
@fill
@lu Et kéint en normalen Dag an der [Woch] sinn, well vill Leit um Busarrêt waarden.
@en It could be a normal day of the [week], because a lot of people are waiting at the bus stop.
@distractor-lu Chrëschtmount
@distractor-lu Stad
@distractor-en December
@distractor-en city

# The uncertain-`ob` pattern, drilled here on the checkable question of rain.
# `ob` sends the verb to the end, an order the learner reads rather than
# assembles; the comma exempts the blank right after it from the Eifeler Regel.
@fill
@lu Ech weess net, ob et [reent].
@en I do not know whether it is [raining].
@distractor-lu schneit
@distractor-lu blénkt
@distractor-en snowing
@distractor-en sparkling

# `bis` ("until") — a natural fit for a bus stop, unlike the earlier themes'
# more generic use of the same connector. Verb-final, so the learner reads the
# order rather than assembling it.
@fill
@lu D'Leit waarden, bis de Bus [kënnt].
@en The people wait until the bus [comes].
@distractor-lu fiert
@distractor-lu geet
@distractor-en drives
@distractor-en goes

# `obwuel` — the harder leading-clause inversion, concession rather than
# condition. The comma ends the clause, which is what keeps the blank after it
# safe under the Eifeler Regel regardless of the preceding word.
@fill
@lu Obwuel den Himmel gro ass, [reent] et net.
@en Although the sky is grey, it is not [raining].
@distractor-lu schneit
@distractor-lu blénkt
@distractor-en snowing
@distractor-en sparkling

# The split `gesäit … aus` hedge, applied to the weather itself rather than a
# person or the scene. The comma before the blank marks the clause boundary and
# exempts it from the Eifeler Regel.
@fill
@lu D'Wieder gesäit [mëll] aus, mee et ass frësch.
@en The weather looks [mild], but it is fresh.
@distractor-lu sonneg
@distractor-lu waarm
@distractor-en sunny
@distractor-en warm

# The new "people at a routine stop" weekday inference, `well`-justified. The
# comma both ends the clause and exempts the blank from the Eifeler Regel; the
# blank is the verb, so a noun cannot fill the slot at all (R3).
@fill
@lu D'Leit sinn um Busarrêt, well si [schaffen].
@en The people are at the bus stop, because they [work].
@distractor-lu spillen
@distractor-lu kafen
@distractor-en play
@distractor-en shop
